only day 3 counts), and the bonus is only +1/day unfertilized. Net
realistic yield = 2 units (initial) + 1 (watering day 3) = 3. So a $20
seed → 3 × $35 = $105 → $85 profit per tile per 3 days. Compare to
wheat: $10 seed → 4 × $25 = $100 → $90 profit per 4 days. Wheat is
better per day despite carrots looking cheaper.
3. Bugs in the latest bot code (not the [cite] one, the "clean" one)
I read the "clean" multi-crop portfolio version. It compiles and runs, but
has several logic problems that are why your score is still plateauing:

Bug 1: crop_age >= maturity_day blocks the "ongoing" crops from ever
being planted correctly
The dispatcher checks t.get("crop") == crop_type in the grid scan and
filters out plants that aren't the currently-selected crop. But crop_type
is the bot's current preferred crop, while existing plants might be from
earlier turns when the rotation picked a different crop. The bot will walk
past, ignore, and let them die. This is why "switching to melons" makes
your carrot field turn to weeds.

Fix: Track the actual crop on each plant tile; water/harvest whatever
is there regardless of crop_type.

Bug 2: seeds[crop_type] -= 1 mutates the seeds dict, but the bot reads
private["seeds"] fresh each turn — so it's actually safe, but only by
accident. If you ever cache seeds in a global, this becomes a real bug.
Bug 3: max_plants counts all plants, not *active plants of the
current crop*. With the rotation logic, the bot thinks it has 40 plants
when it really has 8 carrots + 8 melons (a mix of fast/slow maturing
crops). It keeps buying seeds until total ≥ 40, then stops — but the
problem is that melons tie up tiles for 12 days while the bot wants
to plant tomatoes that mature in 8.
Fix: Count active plants globally; the rotation should plant something
different on every empty tile rather than rotate one crop globally.

Bug 4: BUY_LAND cost is decremented from a local cash variable, not
from farm["money"]. The actual money check happens in
_do_buy_land (server-side) using the real money. So the local
decrement is a no-op accounting trick. Not a bug per se, but it means
the bot's "I have $X" view can drift from the engine's view. If
another market order runs first (sell → +money), the BUY_LAND succeeds;
if it doesn't, BUY_LAND fails silently. The bot never notices.
Bug 5: The seed_cost = CROP_CONFIGS[chosen_crop]["seed"] is computed
and then the gate is cash > cash_guard + seed_cost. That's correct for
not going negative, but there's no gate on "I have enough cash to also
pay the next HIRE and not starve." The HIRE costs come from a separate
Fibonacci counter and aren't subtracted from the cash view either.
Bug 6: [cite: 3] on the continue line and [cite: 1, 2] on
LAND_PRICES — already covered above. The "clean" version at the end of
the log strips these, but it took Gemini two messages to notice. If you
ever paste a Markdown response into a .py file, **search for [cite:
first**.
Bug 7: The bot's grid scan for target = min(harvestable, key=lambda…)
uses Manhattan distance, but the real cost is path cost with locked
tiles. Locked tiles are passable for movement (so a hand can cross them
to reach the other side), but the bot doesn't use the shortcut because
it only ever starts in the NW quadrant. Once it buys the NE quadrant,
the hands still spawn at the center shed-adjacent tiles; crossing from
NW to NE is a 1-tile move, not a 5-tile walk. The bot doesn't exploit
this and will walk the long way around.
Bug 8: The sell_price_offset DNA trait is unitless ($) but the bot
applies it to all crops. The right framing is: sell when price ≥
crop.base × multiplier, where multiplier depends on inventory. For
premium crops (melon, wool, milk, strawberry) the bot should never
sell into a falling market — once a glut starts, price floors at $1
and the offset doesn't help.
Bug 9: Action validation in the engine. The bot returns
{"farmer": [...], "hands": [...], "market": [...]}. The interpreter
accepts the farmer and hands lists as direct action lists, but
only the first 10 market orders are processed. The bot caps at 10
in code (market_queue[:10]) so that's fine, but the inner
_process_market loop iterates outer-index-first over both players'
order queues. That means if both players submit SELL WHEAT 5, the
engine processes 1 unit from each per "tick" — they interleave. Not
your bug, but it means bundling sales across turns beats spamming.
Bug 10: unlocked_quadrants may not exist as a key in early turns.
The code does my_farm.get("unlocked_quadrants", ["NW"]) which is
safe. But it then does len(unlocked_quadrants) - 1 = 0 for
unlocked_extra, and the gate unlocked_extra < target_land_purchases
defaults to target_land_purchases = 3 — so the bot wants to buy 3
expansions as soon as it has $1000. If it doesn't have $4000+500 cash
reserve, it will sit in a loop trying to BUY_LAND. Fine, but each
failed BUY_LAND consumes a market order slot. Wasted slots.
