5. What I'd actually do next
A 5-step plan that doesn't involve grinding a wheat GA against random:

Stop using "random" as the opponent. Train against a pool:
the official "starter" carrot agent, your own bot's previous
champion, and 2-3 of the starter variants with different crops.
Score local matches on win-rate, not in-game cash. Cash is
correlated with skill but not identical; the real leaderboard is
win-rate weighted by opponent rating. Reward the GA on a combined
metric: score = 0.7 * my_cash + 0.3 * (1 if I won else 0).
Add the animal loop to the bot before optimizing further.
Specifically: build 1 coop day 0, buy 1 goose day 0, build 1 pasture
day 0, buy 1 cow day 0, feed both every day, harvest every day. Net
after 30 days: ~26 eggs ($1300) + ~11 milk ($1760) + 60 fertilizer
(worth $1 each via FERTILIZE on a wheat tile) = massive. This is
the comp-skill floor.
Hardcode the price floors. if wheat_price <= 25: hold; if wheat_price >= 45: dump_all. Same for carrot (sell ≥ $42), tomato
(sell ≥ $84), strawberry (sell ≥ $204, never below $80), melon
(sell ≥ $300, never below $150), milk (sell ≥ $256, never below
$100). These are the prices at which one T of supply past I0 has
moved the market by target * base. Below them, you're panic-selling.
Submit one of your 5 daily entries to a fresh account/branch and
let it play ~20 matches before judging. The 600 → 516 score
drop is largely variance, not skill. A bot that wins 8/20 will
rate higher than one that wins 5/20 even if both have similar cash
scores.
