So a few things to clear up before we change strategy:

1. "Watermelon" doesn't exist in this game. The one-time high-value crop is Melon (base $250, $80 seed, 10-12 day maturity, 6 max yield). That's probably what your opponent was running. Strawberries are the other big ongoing one ($120 base, $100 seed, every 2 days).

2. Animals DO sell — and they're not bad. Look at the math:

Cow (milk): $400 cost, base $160, yields 1 milk every 2 days starting day 8, indefinite. Over 22 productive days = 11 milk × $160 = $1,760 ($1,360 profit), plus 22 free fertilizer.
Goose (eggs): $300 cost, base $50, 1 egg/day starting day 4, indefinite. Over 26 productive days = 26 × $50 = $1,300 ($1,000 profit), plus 26 fertilizer.
Sheep (wool): $500 cost, base $200, every 3 days. Lower profit-per-day than cow.
You said "I'm not sure if buying animals is worth it" — it absolutely is. A cow + goose together produce ~$90/day in passive income plus free fertilizer that you can apply to your wheat for 2× yield bonus.

3. "Real Data for farming" — there's no USDA dataset to pull from. The real "data" is the per-step market data in your replays. The brain file you have is already training on that.

4. Buying land is the secret weapon you're missing. Each quadrant is +25% more tiles = +25% more crops = more cash, but it costs $1k + $2k + $4k = $7k total. The bot needs to save up, not spend every dollar on seeds. Once the whole 10×10 board is unlocked with the animal-fertilizer loop, you can run 60+ wheat tiles AND the animals AND tomatoes.

